[Theorem thm:TLC_Holder of arXiv:2605.30999, 'Functional central limit theorems for $U$-statistics of $\beta$-mixing data'] Let $\alpha\in\left(0,1/2\right)$ and define $p\left(\alpha\right):=\left(1/2-\alpha\right)^{-1}$. 
Suppose that (equation eq:condition_sur_beta of the paper) is satisfied as well as the the following conditions:
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{s\to +\infty} s^{p\left(\alpha\right)-1}
    \int_0^1 Q_{h_1\left(X_0\right)}\left(u\right)\mathbf{1}\ens{\alpha^{-1}\left(u\right)Q_{h_1\left(X_0\right)}\left(u\right) >s}du=0
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{t\to +\infty }t^{p\left(\alpha\right)}\max_{k\in \llbracket 1,m\rrbracket }\sup_{\bm{i}\in \operatorname{Inc}^k}
    \mathbb P\pr{\abs{ H_{\bm{i},k}}>t }=0.
\end{equation}
Then the following convergence in distribution takes place: 
 \begin{equation*}
 \pr{ \frac{\sqrt{n}}{\binom nm}\pr{W_{m,n}\left(h,t\right)-\E{W_{m,n}\left(h,t\right)}} }_{0\leqslant t\leqslant 1}
     \to \pr{m\sigma t^{m-1}B_t}_{0\leqslant t\leqslant 1}\mbox{ in }\mathcal{H}_\alpha \left([0,1]\right),
 \end{equation*}
 where $\left(B_t\right)_{0\leqslant t\leqslant 1}$ is a standard Brownian motion and $\sigma^2$ 
 is given by (equation eq:definition_de_sigma of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

