[Proposition 43 of arXiv:2608.09700, 'A general paracontrolled ansatz for singular SPDEs'] We suppose that $u$ can be developed in a paracontrolled system associated with \eqref{eq_gPAM} at order $\gamma$ with random data $\mcZ=(Z_\tau)_{\tau\in\mcT}$. For any $\tau\in\mcT$ and $k\in\bfN^{d+1}$ such that $|k| < \gamma-|\mcI(\tau)|$, we have
		\begin{equation} \begin{split}
			\partial_*^ku_\tau= u_{(\downarrow^k)^* \tau}.
		\end{split}\end{equation}
	For any $k\in\bfN^{d+1}$ with $|k|<\gamma$, 
	\begin{equation} \begin{split}

			\widetilde\partial^k_{*}u = \partial^k_* u.
	\end{split}\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def:log-deriv of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] For $h \in H$, the \emph{logarithmic derivative} of $\mu_t$ along $h$ is the function $\beta_h: H \to \mathbb{R}$ defined $\mu_t$-almost everywhere by the integration-by-parts identity
\begin{equation}
  \int_H \ip{\nabla\phi(u)}{h}_H\,\mathrm{d}\mu_t(u) = -\int_H \phi(u)\,\beta_h(u)\,\mathrm{d}\mu_t(u)
\end{equation}
for all $\phi \in C_b^1(H)$, provided such a $\beta_h \in L^1(\mu_t)$ exists.

