[Thm 2 (REM universality) of arXiv:2604.06122, 'REM universality for linear random energy'] Given $\rho\in (0,1)$, and let $\tilde a$ and $\tilde \lambda$ satisfy
\been{

G^*(\tilde a)=\rho\(\log(2)-\log(1+|m|)\,\),\qquad G'(\tilde \lambda)=\tilde a.
}
Then there exists a $h-$measurable random sequence $(A_n(h))$, such that the point process $\Hn$, defined by
\been{
\Hn(\mathfrak{U}):=\sum_{\sigma\in \Omega_n}\1{U_{\sigma}<\Qs([\sigma_{[n]}])}\1{H_n(h,\sigma)-A_n(h)\in \mathfrak{U}},\quad \mathfrak{U}\in \mathcal{B}(\R),
}
converges in distribution to a PPP with intensity measure $\D_{\tilde \lambda}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

