[Question question-main-3 of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] Let $D\subset\C$ and let $\Phi:D\times D\to\C$ be a map such that for every pair of characteristic functions $f_1,f_2$ on $\R$, the quantity $\Phi(f_1(\xi),f_2(\xi))$ is well defined for every $\xi\in\R$. Let $\cM$ be a subfamily of the set of all characteristic functions on $\R$. Suppose that
    \begin{align}
        \Phi(f_1(\xi),f_2(\xi))\in \cM
        \qquad\text{for all }\xi\in\R.
    \end{align}
    Under what conditions can one conclude that $f_1,f_2\in \cM$? (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2604.08389, 'On a remark of de Gennes concerning three-dimensional polyelectrolytes'] For positive constants $\beta$ and $c<6^{-2/3}$, we have
\begin{equation}
\lim_{T\to\infty}Q_T\bigg(c\beta^{1/3}T(\log T)^{-2/3}
   \le R_T\le 2\cdot 6^{5/6}\beta^{1/3}T(\log T)^{1/3}\bigg) = 1.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:PsiBd of arXiv:2506.06559, 'Bi-infinite incipient cluster in high dimensions'] If (equation eq:tauAsy of the paper), (equation eq:DtauBd of the paper) and (equation eq:Aass of the paper) are satisfied, then for any $F\in\mathfrak F_{00}$ there exits $C>0$ such that for all $x,x'\in\mathbb{Z}^d$ and $p\le p_c$, 
\begin{equation}

	\sum_{N \ge 0} \widetilde{\Psi}^{(N)}(F;x,x')
	   \le C\tau(x)\,\tau(x') \left( \min\{|x|,|x'|,|x-x'|\}\right)^{-(d-4)} 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\sup_{v'}\sum_{N\ge1}\left(
\hat\Psi^{(N,0)}(F;x,x',v') + \sum_{N'\ge1}\Psi^{(N,N')}(F;x,x')\right)\le C 

	\tau(x)\,\tau(x')\,\tau(x-x').
\end{equation} 
\begin{equation}
\sup_v\sum_{N'\ge0}\hat\Psi^{(0,N')}(F;x,v,x') 

	\le C 

	\tau(x)\,\tau(x')\,\tau(x-x').
\end{equation} 
Finally, for all $x,x'\in\mathbb{Z}^d$, 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N,N'\to\infty}R_{N,N'}(F;x,x')=0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition Normal logarithmic derivative identity of arXiv:2608.12376, 'A Heat Kernel Expectation Approach to Boundary-Corrected Li--Yau Estimates for the Dirichl'] For the Dirichlet heat kernel on $\mathbb R^n_+$,

\[
\frac{\partial_{x_n}K_D(x,y,t)}
{K_D(x,y,t)}
=
\frac{y_n}{2t}
\coth
\left(
\frac{x_ny_n}{2t}
\right)
-
\frac{x_n}{2t}.
\]

