[Lemma 6 (Single-edge expansion) of arXiv:2609.03466, 'Least-Favorable Location for Binomial Top-$t$ Selection'] The rate $I_1$ is invariant under $p\mapsto T(p)$ and has its unique minimum at $\pc$. At the center,
\begin{equation}
 \Jn:=I_1(\pc)=-\log(1-\delta^2),
 \qquad
 I_1''(\pc)=\frac{8\delta^2}{(1-\delta^2)^2}.

\end{equation}
On some closed interval $U\subset(0,1-\delta)$ centered at $\pc$, the single-edge probability has the strong expansion
\begin{equation}
 a_n(p)=n^{-1/2}e^{-nI_1(p)}
 \{A_1(p)+o_{C^2(U)}(1)\}.

\end{equation}
Here $A_1(p)$ is smooth, positive, and invariant under $p\mapsto T(p)$. At $p=\pc$,
\begin{align}
 a_n(\pc)
 &=A_{1,\delta}n^{-1/2}e^{-n\Jn}\{1+o(1)\},
 \notag\\
 a_n'(\pc)&=0,
 \\
 a_n''(\pc)
 &=-I_1''(\pc)A_{1,\delta}n^{1/2}e^{-n\Jn}\{1+o(1)\},

\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
 A_{1,\delta}:=A_1(\pc)=\frac1{2\delta\sqrt\pi}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def:poisson_zoo of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Given the $\Gamma$-rotation invariant measure $\nu := \nu_{o}$ and some intensity parameter $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$, let us consider independent Poisson random variables $N_{x,H}^{\lambda} \sim \mathsf{Poisson}( \lambda \cdot \nu_x(H) )$ indexed by $x \in V(G)$ and $H \in \mathcal{H}_x$.
	We say that $N_{x,H}^{\lambda}$ is the number of copies of the animal $H$ (rooted at $x$).
	We call the collection of random variables
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{N}_{\nu}^\lambda := 
		\left\{ 
			N^\lambda_{x,H} \, : \, x \in V(G), H \in \mathcal{H}_x 
		\right\}
	\end{equation}
	the {\bf Poisson zoo} at level $\lambda$ (with measure $\nu$) and the random set
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{S}_{\nu}^\lambda := 
		\bigcup_{x \in V} \bigcup_{H \in \mathcal{H}_x} \bigcup_{i = 1}^{ N_{x,H}^{\lambda} } \, H
	\end{equation}
	the {\bf trace} of the Poisson zoo at level $\lambda$.

[Lemma lemmaNeu1 of arXiv:2606.28067, 'Ergodic approximation for the invariant distribution: An abstract framework for law-depend'] Let $t-s<1$, $x\in{\mathbb R}^+$ and $\mu\in\mathcal{P}_0({\mathbb R}^+)$ (i.e.  a probability measure on ${\mathbb R}_+$). For $q\geq 1$, the following $L^q$ estimates hold:
		\begin{align}
			&\big|{\mathbb E}[\mathcal{X}_{s,r}(x,\mu)^ q]- x^ q\big|\leq C_q(1+x^ q)(t-s)\\
			&{\mathbb E}[|\mathcal{X}_{s,r}(x,\mu)-x|^q]\leq C_q(1+x^ q)(t-s)
		\end{align}
		where $C_q$ depends on $q$, the drift $b$, $J$ and $\|f\|_\infty$.

