        One directly checks for any $\textbf{\textup{C}}^\otimes$, the map ${\mathds{1}}\colon 1\to\textbf{\textup{C}}^\otimes$ classifying the symmetric monoidal unit admits a (unique) symmetric monoidal structure; any other symmetric monoidal functor $1\to\textbf{\textup{C}}^\otimes$ is isomorphic to ${\mathds{1}}$ by the unitality condition for symmetric monoidal functors, and the unitality condition for symmetric monoidal \emph{transformations} implies that this isomorphism is unique. Thus, $\textbf{\textup{Fun}}_\textup{cont}^\otimes(\textbf{\textup{1}},\textbf{\textup{C}}^\otimes)$ is equivalent in the enriched sense (and hence in particular Dwyer--Kan equivalent) to the terminal category for any $\textbf{\textup{C}}^\otimes$, so that $1$ is also initial.

        Finally, we will show that for any $\textbf{\textup{C}}^\otimes,\textbf{\textup{D}}^\otimes,\textbf{\textup{E}}^\otimes$ the map
