{"artifact_digest":"sha256:cec0c6e979375c626bbeff80fd56fb4a326fb9f47037e7320f2341267f5f7827","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ce/cec0c6e979375c626bbeff80fd56fb4a326fb9f47037e7320f2341267f5f7827.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:156eb4ca6b44d68a38d3b07b890ad576ca806bc577c579d8d963b4bd603c9775","bundle_entry_key":"2605-14630-lemma-12-w-inverse","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_032","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_032.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2605-14630-lemma-12-w-inverse","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: For a linear map $g: \R[x]\to\R$, write 
\begin{equation}
 M^g(x^n) = (g\otimes\id) \Delta(x^n)\;.
\end{equation} 
Note that for any linear form $f: \R[x]\to\R$, we have 
\begin{equation}
 \pscal{f}{M^g(x^n)} = (g\otimes f) \Delta(x^n) 
 =: \pscal{g\circ f}{x^n}\;,
\end{equation} 
where we use an \lq\lq inner product\rq\rq\ notation for the action of 
$f$ for clarity. 
It follows that if $h: \R[x]\to\R$ is yet another linear form, then 
\begin{equation}
 \pscal{f}{M^hM^g(x^n)}
 = \pscal{h\circ f}{M^g(x^n)} 
 = \pscal{g\circ h\circ f}{x^n} 
 = \pscal{f}{M^{g\circ h}(x^n)}\;.
\end{equation} 
In short, $M^hM^g = M^{g\circ h}$. Now we observe that 
