[Lemma lem:FP-seed-linear of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The seed functions $\psi_{t,x,a}$ and $\phi_{t,x,a}$
(equation eq:FP-seed-def of the paper) satisfy the linear problem
(equation eq:kpz-red-Lx of the paper)--(equation eq:kpz-red-Lt of the paper) and its
adjoint (equation eq:kpz-red-Ax of the paper)--(equation eq:kpz-red-At of the paper)
with $C = \Lambda = 0$.  Explicitly:
\begin{align}
\partial_x \psi_{t,x,a}
&= \partial_a^2 \psi_{t,x,a},
 \\
\partial_t \psi_{t,x,a}
&= -\tfrac{1}{3}\partial_a^3 \psi_{t,x,a},

\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\partial_x \phi_{t,x,a}
&= -\partial_a^2 \phi_{t,x,a},
 \\
\partial_t \phi_{t,x,a}
&= -\tfrac{1}{3}\partial_a^3 \phi_{t,x,a}.

\end{align}

[Question of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] Is $L(s)=e^{-s}$ characterized among completely monotone functions by the following rigidity property: if
    \begin{align}
        \Phi_L(f_1(\xi),f_2(\xi))=L(a\xi^2)
    \end{align}
    for characteristic functions $f_1$ and $f_2$, then the factors must themselves lie in the image of $L$? (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Prop lem:weyl2 of arXiv:2609.04092, 'Short character sums of inhomogeneous polynomials'] Let $\underline{s}=(s_1,...,s_{k-1})\in \{1,2\}^{k-1}$ where $s_i=1$ for $1\leq i \leq k-(I+1)$ and $s_i=2$ for $k-I\leq i \leq k-1$.
  Then
    \begin{align*}
          |S(\alpha)|^{2^{k-1}}\ll_n  (L_1^n L_2^n)^{2^{k-1} -1}
        (\prod_{i=1}^{k-1} L_{s_i}^n )^{-1} L_{2}^n\sum_{\substack{{\bf h}_1,...,{\bf h}_{k-1} \\ {\bf h}_i\in [-\mathbf{L}_{s_i},\mathbf{L}_{s_i}]}} \prod_{m=1}^n \min \{2L_{1},\|\alpha_{I} C(k) \Psi_m({\bf h}_1,...,{\bf h}_{k-1}) \|^{-1}\},       
    \end{align*}
where $C(k)$ is a nonzero constant depending on $k$ and $\Psi_m$ is defined by
    \begin{align}
         \tilde{\Phi}_{k,(1,1,...,1)}({\bf x},{\bf h}_1,...,{\bf h}_{k-1})=\sum_{m=1}^n x_m \Psi_m({\bf h}_1,...,{\bf h}_{k-1}),
    \end{align}
where recall the definition of $\tilde{\Phi}_{k,(1,1,...,1)}$ from (equation def:tilde_phi of the paper).

