[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2607.09575, 'Probabilistic estimates for a system of\\noisy integrate-and-fire neurons'] For $\delta < \min\{\gamma/4, 1/8C_\sigma^2 T\}$, where $\gamma$ is the constant in \cref{ass: ASSUMPTIONS FROM SOJMARK SPDE PAPER APPLIED TO THE NEUROSCIENCE SETTING} \eqref{ass: ASSUMPTIONS FROM SOJMARK SPDE PAPER APPLIED TO THE NEUROSCIENCE SETTING FOUR}, and any $i = 1,\ldots, N$, there exists a constant $C > 0$ such that
	\begin{equation*}
		\E\left[e^{\delta \hat{\Gamma}_T^i}\right] \le C.
	\end{equation*}
	The constant $C$ depends on $\delta,\,T,\,\mathfrak{K}$, the initial distribution $X_0$, and the bounds on $b$ and $\sigma$, but is independent of $i$ and $N$.

