[Proposition 5 (Triangular tail system) of arXiv:2608.10992, '2608.10992'] Each $q_k$ is non-decreasing and continuously differentiable in $t$,
while $k\mapsto q_k(t)$ is non-increasing. Moreover, $q_1\equiv1$,
$q_k(0)=0$ for $k\geq2$, and
\begin{equation}

        q_k'
        =
        \sum_{u=1}^{k-1}q_u(q_{k-u}-q_k)+q_k(1-q_k),
        \qquad k\geq2.
\end{equation}
In particular, the marginal law of the avalanche range is uniquely determined by this triangular system.

[Corollary of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Assume that 
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto b(t,x,y)\big], \ \big[(t,x)\mapsto \sigma\sigma^\top (t,x,y)\big] \in L^{\infty}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty)\times\mathbb{R}^d),
\end{equation}
uniformly w.r.t. $y\in \mathbb{R}$.
Then, D-MKV$(b,\sigma)$ is weakly well-posed if and only if D-NL-MP$(b,\sigma;\nu)$ admits a unique solution for any $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:IBJ-S2-inv of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] For all $x, y \in \mathcal{V}^m$,
\begin{equation}
B_{\mathcal{S}_2 u}
(\mathcal{S}_2 x, \mathcal{S}_2 y)
= B_u(x, y).
\end{equation}

[Definition of arXiv:2607.14792, 'On the degree distribution of large tentacular Bienaymé--Galton--Watson trees'] Let $\mu$ be an offspring distribution on $\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ and $(C_{u})_{u\in \mathcal{U}}$ be independent and indentically distributed random variables of law $\mu$. A random tree is a  Bienaymé–Galton–Watson tree with offspring distribution $\mu$ ($\mu$-BGW tree) if it has the same law as the random tree $T$ defined by \[T=\left\{u=(u_1,\dots,u_m)\in\mathcal U:
u_j\le C_{(u_1,\dots,u_{j-1})}
\text{ for every }1\le j\le m\right\}.\]

[Lemma lem:at_1 of arXiv:2603.27906, 'Marked GUE-corners process in doubly periodic dimer models'] The following identities hold:
\begin{equation}
Q(q_\infty)=\frac{1}{1+\beta_\ell^{-1} \alpha_1}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
\alpha_1
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & \beta_\ell^{-1}
\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\left.(z-1)^\ell w\right|_{(z,w)=q_{\infty}}=\prod_{m=1}^\ell(1+\alpha_m^{-1}\beta_m)(1+\alpha_{m+1}\beta_m^{-1}),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\left.(z-1)^{-\ell} w\right|_{(z,w)=q_{0}}=\left(\prod_{m=1}^\ell(1+\alpha_m^{-1}\beta_m)(1+\alpha_{m+1}\beta_m^{-1})\right)^{-1},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left.(z-1)^{i-i'}\prod_{m=2i'+1}^{2i}\phi_m(z)\right|_{z=1}
=\prod_{m=i'+1}^i(1+\alpha_m^{-1}\beta_m)\prod_{m=i'+1}^{i-1}(1+\alpha_{m+1}\beta_m^{-1})
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
\alpha_{i'+1}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & \beta_i^{-1}
\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
for~$0\leq i'<i\leq \ell$.

