[Definition 42 ($2$ shift operator) of arXiv:2604.04267, 'On Sharpest Tail Bounds for Functions \\ of Tail Bounded Random Variables'] The $2$ shift operator $\mathscr{S}_2$ is defined as
\begin{align*}
   \mathscr{S}_2: (f,G,c) \mapsto (X_1,...,X_n)
\end{align*} 
where $f = \big((f_1^-,f_1^+),...,(f_n^-,f_n^+)\big) \in \mathcal{B}_2^n, \ G \ni 0$ and is closed, $c=(c_1,...,c_n) \in [0,\infty]^n$, and for $k=1,...,n$, 
$X_k:(0,1) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that
\begin{equation}
X_k(s) :=
\begin{cases}
   \mathscr{S}_L(f_k^-, G^k)(s) & 0 < s < c_k \\ 
   \mathscr{S}_R(f_k^+, G^k)(s) & c_k \leq s < 1
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

