[OPEN PROBLEM - not a proved fact. Stated in arXiv:2607.04467, 'Bernstein Functions at Work: Coalescents, Copulas, and Subordination' (problem environment labelled prob:townes).] For \(\eta=10^{-4}\), set
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
L(s)=\exp\!\Bigl(&-10\eta s+10^{-4}(e^{-10\eta s}-1)\\
&\quad +(e^{\eta s}-1)\Bigr),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
the bilateral Laplace transform of \(X=\eta(10+10P-Q)\), where \(P\) and \(Q\)
are independent and
\begin{equation}
P\sim\Pois(10^{-4}),\qquad Q\sim\Pois(1).
\end{equation}
Supply a complete certificate that \(L\) is completely monotone on \([0,1]\),
while
\(L^{1/100}\) is not completely monotone at \(s=0\). This would produce a real
ID mixing law whose mixed Poisson law is not \(100\)-divisible. (Verbatim from the paper's TeX; symbols are the source paper's. Stated as open/conjectural; no resolution of it was found in the paper's own text by the rules of problem_index.py - a paper may still prove special cases of a conjecture it states.)

