[Prop 0108390 of arXiv:2606.29143, 'Comonotonic and moment matching approximations for sums of lognormal random variables'] Let $A = (a_1, ..., a_m)^{'}$ and $Q = (q_1, ..., q_m)^{'}$ be two numerical vectors with $\min(a_1, ..., a_m) \geq 0, \max(a_1, ..., a_m) > 0$ and $0 = q_0 < q_1 < q_2 < ... < q_m = 1$. Then, for any continuous random variable $X$ the stop-loss premiums of the step-weighted distribution associated with the numerical vectors $A$ and $Q$ are given by  
\begin{align*}
E[(X_{A, Q} - d)_{+}] &= {a}^{-1}\Big[a_lE[(X - d)_{+}] + d\Big(a_l(1 - q_{l}) - \sum^{m - 1}_{k = l}a_{k + 1}(q_{k + 1} - q_{k})\Big)  \\ 
&\quad + \sum^{m - 1}_{k = l}(a_{k + 1} - a_{k})(1 - q_k)\text{TVaR}_{q_k}[X]\Big], \,\, d \in (F^{-1+}_{X_{A, Q}}(0), F^{-1}_{X_{A, Q}}(1))
\end{align*}
with $F_X(d) \in (q_{l - 1}, q_l]$ for some $l \in \{1, ..., m\}$ and $\sum_{k = m}^{m - 1} = 0$ by convention.

