body:
[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2607.22438, 'A deterministic scalar reduction for the phase transition in two interacting urns'] For each \(z\in(1/2,3/4)\), the equation
\begin{equation*}
 \widetilde F_p(t,z)=0
\end{equation*}
has a unique solution \(q_p(z)\) in \((1/2,1)\). This solution lies on the decreasing part of the graph \(t\mapsto\widetilde F_p(t,z)\), and this solution satisfies \(q_p(z)>z\). Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 D_p(z):=\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\widetilde F_p(q_p(z),z)
 =-1+(1-p)h'(q_p(z))<0.
\end{equation}
The function \(q_p\) is analytic on \((1/2,1)\) and extends analytically to a neighborhood of \(z=1/2\).

